## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and ABA avoidance in a tiny mailbox primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic CAS on a 64-bit slot; sequence counters prevent ABA.
- Memory_order_release on publish, memory_order_acquire on receive for visibility.
- Fill-empty state via payload==0 heuristic for simplicity; robust designs can reserve a dedicated marker.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { _Atomic uint64_t slot; } Mailbox;
void mailbox_init(Mailbox *m){ atomic_init(&m->slot, 0); }
bool mailbox_send(Mailbox *m, uint32_t payload){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint64_t want = ((uint64_t)seq<<32);
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | payload;
  return atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &want, next);
}
bool mailbox_recv(Mailbox *m, uint32_t *out){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_acquire);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint32_t payload = (uint32_t)(cur & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  if (payload == 0) return false;
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | 0u;
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &cur, next)){
    *out = payload; return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers while keeping SPSC guarantees?
- What microarchitectural pitfalls exist (false sharing, cache lines) and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory-management skills: implementing per-thread caching, lock-free global pools, ABA avoidance, and handling thread cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- **Thread-local caches**: Minimize contention by keeping allocations per-thread
- **Lock-free free-list**: Use CAS (compare-and-swap) for thread-safe global pool access
- **ABA avoidance**: Tagged pointers or epoch-based schemes prevent the ABA problem where a recycled pointer is misinterpreted as valid
- **Burst refills**: Refill local caches in batches to reduce global pool access frequency
- **Cache locality**: Keeping objects per-thread improves CPU cache hit rates
- **Safe cleanup**: Handle releasing per-thread buffers back to the global pool when threads exit

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; char pad[56]; } Block;

typedef struct { _Atomic(Block *) head; } FreeList;

static inline void* pool_alloc(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt) {
    if (*lcnt == 0) {
        Block *batch[32];
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_acquire);
        do { for (int i = 0; i < 31; i++) batch[i] = batch[i]->next; } 
        while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, batch[31]));
        *lcnt = 31;
        return batch[0];
    }
    return local[--*lcnt];
}

static inline void pool_free(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt, void *ptr) {
    if (*lcnt == 32) {
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_relaxed);
        do { local[31] = head; } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, local[0]));
        *lcnt = 0;
    }
    local[(*lcnt)++] = ptr;
}
```

